{
 "id": "2024-lis-lighting",
 "name": "Lighting Features of a Park in the Evening",
 "description": "Faithful replication of the online survey in Lis, Zienowicz & Błachnio (2024), \"Lighting Features Affecting the Well-Being of Able-Bodied People and People with Physical Disabilities in the Park in the Evening: An Integrated and Sustainable Approach to Lighting Urban Green Areas\", Sustainability 16(20):8871. Twelve SketchUp/Lumion renderings of the same park path at night follow a 2 × 3 × 2 design: light distribution (even, uneven) × lighting location (path, path + surroundings, surroundings) × space (open, corridor). After a screening question (a negative or traumatic experience in a park ends the survey), age and gender, participants rate all 12 scenes in five parts, one scene per slide in random order, on safety, preference, contact with the surroundings, mystery and legibility (1–5, wording from Table 1). Answers are keyed by image: letter A–F = lighting (A even/path, B even/path + surroundings, C even/surroundings, D uneven/path, E uneven/path + surroundings, F uneven/surroundings), digit 1 = open space, 2 = corridor space. The paper (N = 193; 95 wheelchair users, 98 able-bodied people, Poland) found evenly lit scenes rated higher than spot-lit ones, and that the best lighting location differed between the two groups. Stimuli: the 12 renderings cropped from Figure 3 of the paper (CC BY 4.0). The template will be removed if the authors object. Differences from the original study: (1) only the wheelchair-user image set (eye height 1.20 m) is printed in the paper, so everyone sees that set; the able-bodied group originally saw the same scenes from 1.65 m; (2) one survey with a \"Do you use a wheelchair?\" question replaces the two separate surveys; (3) the order of the five parts follows Table 1 (safety, preference, contact, mystery, legibility), which the paper does not state; (4) you can go back within a part but not to a finished part.",
 "author": "Lis, A., Zienowicz, M., & Błachnio, A.",
